'''Eileen Feng Gu''' (born September 3, 2003), also known by her [[Chinese name#In English|Chinese name]] '''Gu Ailing''' ({{lang-zh|s=谷爱凌|labels=no}}), is a Chinese-American [[freestyle skier]] and model<!--Details of life covered in subsequent sentence.-->.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lipin |first=Michael |date=2022-02-11 |title=Exclusive: Olympic Websites Scrub Conflicting Citizenship Info on China’s Star Winter Athlete |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/exclusive-olympic-websites-scrub-conflicting-citizenship-info-on-china-s-star-winter-athlete-/6438850.html |access-date=2026-02-24 |website=Voice of America |language=en}}</ref> She has represented China in [[Half-pipe skiing|halfpipe]], [[slopestyle]], and [[big air]] events since the 2018–19 season. With three gold and three silver medals, Gu is the most decorated freestyle skier in [[Freestyle skiing at the Winter Olympics|Olympic history]].

Gu was born and raised in the United States. She began competing for China in 2019. At age 18, she became the youngest Olympic champion in freestyle skiing after winning gold medals in [[Freestyle skiing at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Women's big air|big air]] and [[Freestyle skiing at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Women's halfpipe|halfpipe]], and a silver medal in [[Freestyle skiing at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Women's slopestyle|slopestyle]], at the [[2022 Winter Olympics]] in Beijing. She is the first freestyle skier to win three medals at a single Winter Olympics.

At the [[2026 Winter Olympics]] in Milan-Cortina, Gu won gold in women's halfpipe and two additional silver medals in big air and slopestyle, setting the Olympic medal record in freestyle skiing, surpassing all previous competitors regardless of gender.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Eileen Gu defends halfpipe gold, leaves Milan Cortina with 3 medals {{!}} NBC Olympics |url=https://www.nbcolympics.com/news/eileen-gu-defends-halfpipe-gold-leaves-milan-cortina-3-medals |access-date=2026-02-22 |website=www.nbcolympics.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.olympics.com/en/milano-cortina-2026/results/athlete-details/40014 |title=Eileen Gu |work=[[Milano Cortina 2026 Olympics]] |access-date=February 21, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260209194337/https://www.olympics.com/en/milano-cortina-2026/results/athlete-details/40014 |archive-date=February 9, 2026 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' named Gu as one of the 100 most influential people in the world under its Pioneers category on its [[Time 100|annual list]] in 2022.<ref name=":7">{{Cite magazine |date=May 23, 2022 |title=Eileen Gu is on the 2022 Time 100 List |url=https://time.com/collection/100-most-influential-people-2022/6177852/eileen-gu/ |url-status=live |access-date=June 20, 2022 |magazine=Time |archive-date=February 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230212223442/https://time.com/collection/100-most-influential-people-2022/6177852/eileen-gu/ }}</ref> She was subsequently featured on the magazine's February 9, 2026, edition.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gregory |first=Sean |date=January 22, 2026 |title='I Don't Believe in Limits.' How Eileen Gu Became Freestyle Skiing's Biggest Star |url=https://time.com/7355691/eileen-gu-interview-2026-olympics/ |website=Time.com |edition=02.09.2026}}</ref> She was the fourth-highest earning female athlete in the world in 2025.<ref name="harpur">{{cite news |last=Harpur |first=Charlotte |title=Eileen Gu: The Winter Olympian who earns $23m a year — but just $100k of it from her sport |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7003969/2026/02/05/eileen-gu-winter-olympics-23million/ |access-date=16 February 2026 |work=The Athletic, The New York Times |date=5 February 2026}}</ref>

Gu won the [[Laureus World Sports Awards|Laureus World Action Sportsperson of the Year]] in 2023. She also co-hosted the 2026 Laureus World Sports Awards alongside [[Novak Djokovic]]. This marks the first time that two athletes hosted the [[Laureus World Sports Awards]].

==Early life and education==
Eileen Gu was born on September 3, 2003, in [[San Francisco]], California, United States.<ref name=":3">{{cite web |title=Eileen Gu |url=https://www.redbull.com/us-en/athlete/eileen-gu |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210908213914/https://www.redbull.com/us-en/athlete/eileen-gu |archive-date=8 September 2021 |access-date=12 March 2021 |website=Red Bull}}</ref> Her mother, Yan Gu ({{lang-zh|c=谷燕|p=Gǔ Yàn|links=no}}), is a [[first-generation immigrant|first-generation]] Chinese immigrant to the United States. Her father is American and a [[Harvard University]] graduate.<ref name="branch" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-02-09 |title=Freestyle Skier Eileen Gu's Mom Has Supported Her Olympic Dreams Since Day One—More About Her Family |url=https://www.womenshealthmag.com/life/a39003028/eileen-gu-parents-father-family/ |access-date=2026-02-21 |website=Women's Health |language=en-US}}</ref> Her maternal grandfather was the chief electrical engineer of the [[Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development|Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development of China]].<ref name="branch">{{Cite news |last=Branch |first=John |date=2022-02-03 |title=Eileen Gu Is Trying to Soar Over the Geopolitical Divide |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/03/sports/olympics/eileen-gu-china-freeski.html |access-date=2022-05-04 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=February 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220210171756/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/03/sports/olympics/eileen-gu-china-freeski.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Yan studied chemistry and biochemistry and was a member of the [[short-track speed skating]] team at [[Peking University]] in the 1980s. After moving to the United States, she earned a [[master's degree]] in biochemistry and molecular biology  at [[Auburn University]] and an [[MBA]]  at the [[Stanford University Graduate School of Business]].<ref name="branch" /><ref>{{cite news |date=9 February 2022 |title=Winter Olympics: Chinese freestyle ski star Eileen Gu's mother gives advice to parents hoping to ra.ise a champion |work=South China Morning Post |url=https://www.scmp.com/news/people-culture/china-personalities/article/3166399/winter-olympics-chinese-freestyle-ski-star |access-date=10 February 2022 |archive-date=February 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220210125402/https://www.scmp.com/news/people-culture/china-personalities/article/3166399/winter-olympics-chinese-freestyle-ski-star |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Qin |first=Amy |date=2022-02-13 |title=After a reply on Instagram, Eileen Gu faces criticism over 'special treatment.' |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/12/sports/olympics/eileen-gu-instagram-vpn-china.html |access-date=2022-06-10 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=June 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220610235715/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/12/sports/olympics/eileen-gu-instagram-vpn-china.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Yan raised her daughter as a single mother in San Francisco's [[Sea Cliff, San Francisco|Sea Cliff]] neighborhood.<ref name="branch"/> At three years old, Gu began skiing in Lake Tahoe, where Yan once worked as a part-time ski instructor. She joined the [[Northstar California]] Resort free-ski team at eight and won her first national championship at nine.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Eileen Gu: Watch Everyday Eileen freeskiing TV show |url=https://www.redbull.com/us-en/eileen-gu-career-personal-life-portrait |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=Red Bull |date=December 15, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=March 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230306022308/https://www.redbull.com/us-en/eileen-gu-career-personal-life-portrait |url-status=live }}</ref>

Gu attended primary and middle school at the [[Katherine Delmar Burke School]] and went to high school at [[San Francisco University High School|San Francisco University High]]. She attended a mathematics [[Buxiban|cram school]] during her summers in Beijing<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-02-18 |title=How citizenship row clouded Eileen Gu's Olympics |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/18/how-citizenship-row-clouded-eileen-gus-olympics-chinese-american-skier |access-date=2022-05-29 |website=The Guardian |language=en |archive-date=May 29, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220529052714/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/18/how-citizenship-row-clouded-eileen-gus-olympics-chinese-american-skier |url-status=live }}</ref> and scored 1580 out of 1600 on  the [[SAT]].<ref name="FIS"/>

Gu earned early admittance to [[Stanford University]]  in December 2020.<ref name="Paul_LifestyleAsia"/> She entered the freshman class in 2022,<ref>{{cite web |last = Murphy |first = Bryan |date = 7 February 2022 |title = All About Eileen Gu, the Chinese-American Olympic Freestyle Skier – NBC New York |url = https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/sports/beijing-winter-olympics/all-about-eileen-gu-the-chinese-american-olympic-freestyle-skier/3538912/ |access-date = 9 February 2022 |website = NBC New York |archive-date = February 8, 2022 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20220208172410/https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/sports/beijing-winter-olympics/all-about-eileen-gu-the-chinese-american-olympic-freestyle-skier/3538912/ |url-status = live }}</ref><ref name="Paul_LifestyleAsia"/> the same year she was presented as a [[debutante]] at [[Le Bal des débutantes]] in Paris.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Bal des Débutantes is Back at the Shangri-La Paris Hotel |url=https://airmail.news/issues/2023-2-11/a-stylish-entrance |access-date=2024-02-21 |website=Airmaillanguage=en}}</ref> While at Stanford, she joined [[Kappa Kappa Gamma]] sorority and studied abroad at the [[University of Oxford]]'s [[Magdalen College, Oxford|Magdalen College.]]<ref>{{cite web |last= Becque|first= Fran|date= 4 February 2026|title= Fraternity and Sorority Members Competing in the 2026 Winter Olympics|url= https://www.franbecque.com/fraternity-and-sorority-members-competing-in-the-2026-winter-olympics/|website= Fraternity History & More|location= |publisher= |access-date= 9 February 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Savino |first1=Christine |title=Former Oxford student makes history at Winter Olympics |url=https://www.oxfordstudent.com/2026/02/23/former-oxford-student-makes-history-at-winter-olympics/ |work=The Oxford Student}}</ref> An [[international relations]] major, Gu took time off in the 2025–26 academic year to prepare for the [[2026 Winter Olympics]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Meet the Stanford undergrads heading to the Winter Olympics |url=https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2026/02/stanford-undergraduates-sammy-smith-eileen-gu-zoe-atkin-brandon-kim-winter-olympics-primer |access-date=2026-02-09 |website=news.stanford.edu |language=en}}</ref> In June 2026, Gu graduated from Stanford University.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.powder.com/news/eileen-gu-attends-stanford-commencement-ceremony|title=Eileen Gu Attends Stanford Commencement Ceremony|work=Powder Magazine|first=Izzy|last=Lidsky|date=June 15, 2026|access-date=June 15, 2026}}</ref>

== Sports career ==
[[File:2020-01-20 Freestyle skiing at the 2020 Winter Youth Olympics – Women's Halfpipe – Qualification – 2nd run (Martin Rulsch) 027.jpg|thumb|Gu's second run in the Women's Freeski Halfpipe Qualification at the [[2020 Winter Youth Olympics]]]]
[[File:Gu Ailing Eileen on her first run at Women's Freeski Big Air Qualification (20220207093705).jpg|thumb|Gu's first run at Women's Freeski Big Air Qualification, 2022 Olympics]]In 2021, Gu became the first woman to land a forward double cork 1440 in competition history.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://olympics.com/en/news/china-s-eileen-gu-stomps-world-s-first-women-s-freeski-double-cork-1440|title=China's Eileen Gu stomps world's first women's freeski double cork 1440|access-date=10 November 2021|archive-date=10 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211110050653/https://olympics.com/en/news/china-s-eileen-gu-stomps-world-s-first-women-s-freeski-double-cork-1440|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Coaches and training ===
Gu's coaches have included Jamie Melton, head coach of the Chinese National Slopestyle and Big Air Training Team for the 2022 Winter Olympics,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Colbert |first=Austin |title=New star Eileen Gu wins second gold, third medal in historic X Games Aspen debut |url=https://www.aspentimes.com/news/new-star-eileen-gu-wins-second-gold-third-medal-in-historic-x-games-aspen-debut/ |access-date=2022-09-23 |website=Aspen Times |date=January 31, 2021 |language=en-US |archive-date=September 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220923001851/https://www.aspentimes.com/news/new-star-eileen-gu-wins-second-gold-third-medal-in-historic-x-games-aspen-debut/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and Brad Prosser, who met Gu when she was ten. In 2018, he became the technical coach guide to the Chinese national team for the 2022 Winter Olympics. Her personal coach for the 2022 Olympics was Misra Noto Torniainen, the former coach of the Swiss freeski team.<ref>{{cite web |date=2022-02-01 |title=Olympics freeski star Eileen Gu's delicate balancing act between China and the U.S. |url=https://www.espn.com/olympics/story/_/id/33160624/olympics-2022-freeski-star-eileen-gu-delicate-balancing-act-china-us |access-date=2022-05-05 |website=ESPN |language=en |archive-date=February 1, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220201183344/https://www.espn.com/olympics/story/_/id/33160624/olympics-2022-freeski-star-eileen-gu-delicate-balancing-act-china-us |url-status=live }}</ref> Torniainen coached Olympic medalists [[Sarah Höfflin]] and [[Mathilde Gremaud]] for the [[2018 Winter Olympics]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Anzeiger Bezirk Affoltern: Gold-Trainer aus Mettmenstetten |url=https://www.affolteranzeiger.ch/aktuell/sport/artikelseite-sport/?tx_ttnews%5Btt_news%5D=91005&cHash=a8adbc01dea633d17ed2e62ce6ad94aa |access-date=2022-06-13 |website=Affolter Anzeiger |archive-date=July 28, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220728220239/https://www.affolteranzeiger.ch/ak